Z||000003||PBJ||05-16-2001||07:18 PM||||I can get 250 k out of a tank of gas from a full service station - BUT - if I fill it and do the click click click X 10  I can easily now get over 300 k out of a tank.  it does take time to choke it in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||05-16-2001||09:03 PM||||My 88 runs out of gas at 1/4 tank, then takes only 10 gallons to fill.  I suspect that however the fuel pump sucks the gas out of the tank is positioned above the level of gas so that it stops drawing gas when there is 1.5 gallons left in the tank which is a little less than 1/4 tank. Gas guages are notoriously inaccurate.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||05-16-2001||09:14 PM||||The fuel pickup on the pump sits on top of part of the baffle also,not directly on the bottom of the tank. I also noticed going down a steep drive that I lost fuel with a little over a 1/4 tank showing. pick up is at the rear and it is a long tank.||||reg||1
Z||000006||phreakboy4||05-16-2001||09:18 PM||||my 84 2M4 coupe wont take over  8 gallons no matter what i try to squeeze in after it shuts off||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||05-16-2001||09:35 PM||||I noticed that the pumps kick off way early too.  Not like I can trust my gauge though, but the only time I can be for sure that my tank is full is when I go click click click until gas spills out onto the ground  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I've only done that once, but does anyone know why it does this?  Is it because it is such a small tank and pressure gets built up in there easier to make the pump kick off?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bluebottlejunky||05-16-2001||09:39 PM||||hmmm, how dose a pump know when to stop anyway? hehe just wondering  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp, <BR>AOL IM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000009||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-16-2001||09:44 PM||||Don't overfill your tank!<P>Gas will travel back the canister vent pipe and kill it.  Then, the car will be much harder to start.<P>I did this to a 2000 Honda XR650L to squeeze a little more into the 2.5 gallon tank.  Then it would backfire everytime I tried to start it up.<P>Let the pump stop the gas.  It is sense the pressure change in the tank.  When it senses that the fuel is getting close to the filler neck, it stops.  This is good as the canister will never get fouled.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||12:07 AM||||What are you pissed about?! I had a dodge van that had a 22 gallon tank. I almost ran it out of gas in a small town about 20 miles south of the city, so I filled it up in a station there.<P>  It took 25.3 gallons, in a 22 gallon tank! I went inside and asked to see the manager. I told him, "we need to talk dude!"<P>  He only charged me for 20 gallons and gave me some BS about the pump needed to be calibrated.<P>  I was pissed!||||reg||1
Z||000011||RaidenCobra||05-17-2001||01:33 AM||||Hahah..<BR>I guess i should start paying attention to that.. I swear i got ripped off a couple times||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||05-17-2001||05:10 AM||||theres always some left in tank that pump wont pick up. will differ from car to car because of slight variations in mounting. Those pumps shut off filling when they 'sniff' the fumes. It will go longer before it kicks off automaticly if you fill it slowly...the fumes wont back up so much. If your tank is low dont park on inclines. Even if you have a 1/2 tank of gas, if the incline is steep enough the car wont pick it up.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||05-17-2001||08:24 AM||||I friend of mine has been trying to figure out the same thing. He says he used to get 200 miles out of a full tank of fuel but now only gets about 160. He pulled his tank and noticed the plastic splash baffle was split in multiple locations. This baffle is designed to hold around the fuel pickup and prevent starvation under hard acceleration, braking, or cornering. Since it was split, the fuel would drain out too quickly and give the appearance of being out or low on fuel. <P>I notice on mine, the split must be in the front. When I get to about 140 miles, I can hear the fuel pump suck air under hard braking. But, the car runs and sounds normal when I accelerate. <P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||09:47 AM||||The second time I filled my fiero up I wanted to start tracking the mileage, so I wanted to fillet right to the neck.<P>the pump kicked off after 6 gallons went in, so I squeezed the handle and started doing the click click click thing.<P>another gallon went in and still no fuel in the filler neck. <P>at 7.5 gallons I started to wonder if fuel was coming out somewhere, so I stopped and looked under the car and in the engine compartment (I really expected to see a puddle of gas under the car).<P>at 8.5 gallons I was starting to think the gas must be going into the frame, or inside the passenger compartment, really starting to worry, then it finally filled the neck.<P>its gotta be the shape of the gas tank, maybe air get trapped in the front and has to bubble up the filler neck. All it takes is the slightest backpressure on the nozzel to trip the shutoff.<P>while we are talking about this, here is a slick trick: when you are tanking up, put your gas cap in the pump handle to hold it on, then you dont have to stand there and hold it looking like a dope - it will shut off long before it overflows.<P>  Esp nice in the winter when you forgot your gloves.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||09:51 AM||||BTW, im not saying my tank holds 8.5 gallons - it wasnt empty when I started.<P>another thought on questionable gas gauges. You can get a one gallon plastic gas can from Kmart for about $2. If your gauge is questionable keep a gallon in your front or back trunk.<P>  you wont have to worry about it so much - kinda like the reserve tank on a motorcycle.<P>  and if you see some babe on the side of the road, out of gas, you get to play Knight on a white Fiero hero dude!||||reg||1
Z||000016||jerpro||05-17-2001||04:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>I friend of mine has been trying to figure out the same thing. He says he used to get 200 miles out of a full tank of fuel but now only gets about 160. He pulled his tank and noticed the plastic splash baffle was split in multiple locations. This baffle is designed to hold around the fuel pickup and prevent starvation under hard acceleration, braking, or cornering. Since it was split, the fuel would drain out too quickly and give the appearance of being out or low on fuel.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>hmmm.... ho wexactly can I check for this?<BR>what procedure should I follow to get the tank of gas out?<P>and BTW ken, I [B]strongly[B/] advise against keeping a reservoir of gas in you car. You might bring back the "fad" of burning fieros!<P>I will try the click click thing next time I fill up. Let's see how much I can fill in. hehehe....<P><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if at first you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||04:46 PM||||yes carting a can of gas around does have some risk. I think up front with a bungee cord to hold it down might be the best way to go.<P> or at least keep an empty plastic gas can in the car. Then when you walk into the gas station with it you can pretend you live next store and its for your lawn mower, and maintain some of your dignity (c:  &lt;- i like this face - thats cool!||||reg||1
Z||000018||EdsB52||05-17-2001||05:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>yes carting a can of gas around does have some risk. I think up front with a bungee cord to hold it down might be the best way to go.<P> or at least keep an empty plastic gas can in the car. Then when you walk into the gas station with it you can pretend you live next store and its for your lawn mower, and maintain some of your dignity (c:  &lt;- i like this face - thats cool!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, what you do is buy a gallon of water when you get to the convenience store, dump it out after you drink half due to extreme thirst from your five-mile walk, and fill it up with gas! I doubt I'm the first to think of this.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||EdsB52||05-17-2001||05:34 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>My 88 runs out of gas at 1/4 tank, then takes only 10 gallons to fill.  I suspect that however the fuel pump sucks the gas out of the tank is positioned above the level of gas so that it stops drawing gas when there is 1.5 gallons left in the tank which is a little less than 1/4 tank. Gas guages are notoriously inaccurate.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In aviation they call this "useable fuel" and factor it in when planning trips. They are aware that not all fuel is reachable, hence unuseable and specifically differentiate.<p>[This message has been edited by EdsB52 (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||05-17-2001||10:24 PM||||Yeah, I know about filling up a water bottle with gas. I ran outta gas with my old Bronco, and had to make 6 trips with a little 1 ltr. bottle of gas each time just to get it started. I then drove to the gas station and it coasted into it, thank god there was pump open right there.||||reg||1
Z||000021||pontiacs4me||05-18-2001||01:02 AM||||After reading all of the replies on this thread, I think I figured out the answer to the question of why the pump clicks, and you can squeeze off 2 more gallons.  Being the odd shape of the gas tank, with the filler tube at the rear, with the top of the filler located 3 inches from the top of the tank itself.  When you pump the gas, and it fills the tank to the top of the filler tube, it fills the tube real quick, and shuts off the pump.  Most of the time it shoots about 2 ounces out and on my paintjob.  Then, the fuel that is in the filler tube, drains into the bubble of air that is above the top of the filler tube.  There is a vent tube at the top of the tank, in the sending unit assembly.  The bubble of air vents out, as the fuel in the filler tube drains in.  At that point you can squeeze off another 40 cents in fuel.  Then it backs up again, and it clicks again.  If you pump slowly, the bubble of air vents and it fills without clicking.  The tank will finally fill, and the filler neck will fill, then you will be ready to cruise.  <BR>Hope my ameteur description makes sense.||||reg||1
Z||000022||FieroJoe||05-18-2001||10:49 AM||||I wonder if the secret fuel vapor tank thing located inside the right side rear quarter panel has anything to do with this....<P>Does that thing fill up with fuel before the vapor canister?  <I>Can</I> it fill up with fuel??<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||11:00 AM||||I tanked my car up this morning - didnt fill it up all the way - and now I smell gas inside the car again.<P>  there must either be a poorly connected vent tube on my tank, or there must be a leak onthe top of my gas tank.<P>  Time to buy somemore JB weld  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||Fierohoho'||05-18-2001||02:51 PM||||Try this, <BR>lift the pump handle so the nozzle points down into the tank instead of at the back of the filler tube and don't put the nozzle all the way into the filler tube.<P>If you look at the underside of the end of the filler nozzleyou will see a small hole, if you look into the end of the pump nozzle you will see a tube connected to this hole. When the fuel (gas for you ricers) backs up into the filler neck it gets into the hole which "plugs" and trips the handle off.||||reg||1
